WEBVTT

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:08.220
<v Ira Glass>Hey everybody. Ira Glass here. So we got this email at our radio show near the end of the last school year from a 14-year-old.
<v Ira Glass>大家好，我是艾拉·格拉斯。上个学年末，我们广播节目的编辑部收到了一封邮件，发件人是一位14岁的学生。

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:09.220
<v Annie>Hello?
<v Annie>喂？

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:09.930
<v Ira Glass>Hey, is this Annie?
<v Ira Glass>嘿，是安妮吗？

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:10.680
<v Annie>Yes.
<v Annie>是的。

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:14.160
<v Ira Glass>I called her up at her house in California and asked her to read it.
<v Ira Glass>我给她加州的家里打了电话，请她读了这封邮件。

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:34.060
<v Annie>It says, "Dear This American Life, I just escaped the whitewashed, brick-walled, iron-gated prison that is commonly known as middle school, and I'm finally out for good. But in all the time I've listened to your show, I've never heard an episode devoted to what goes on inside the walls of a middle school. I hope you'll think about it. Anonymous."
<v Annie>上面写着：“亲爱的《美国生活》栏目，我刚刚逃离了那个被称为初中的、粉刷着白墙、砖墙铁门的监狱，我终于彻底解脱了。但在我收听你们节目的这段时间里，我从没听过哪一期节目专门讲初中围墙里到底发生了什么。我希望你们能考虑一下。——匿名者。”

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:39.040
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, you signed it anonymous, but then your email was signed with your name.
<v Ira Glass>是的，你署名是“匿名者”，但你的邮件后面却签了你的名字。

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:40.390
<v Annie>Yeah.
<v Annie>是的。

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:41.320
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:54.960
<v Annie>I did anonymous because in middle school, everybody is so judgmental, and I didn't want the kids to judge me or anything if they heard me on the radio.
<v Annie>我之所以用匿名，是因为在初中，每个人都特别爱评头论足，如果同学们在广播里听到我的声音，我不想让他们对我评头论足。

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:05.720
<v Ira Glass>Mainly, she says that she wrote to us because she and her friends were talking right after they left eighth grade about how terrible middle school was. And she wondered was it just as bad for other people as it was for them?
<v Ira Glass>她主要说，她给我们写信是因为她和朋友们在刚离开八年级后就在谈论初中有多么糟糕。她想知道，这对其他人来说是否也和她们一样糟糕？

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:16.180
<v Annie>You always wonder whether other people are going through the same thing as you. And it'd be cool to hear other people's stories about it and what they went through.
<v Annie>你总是会想，别人是否也在经历和你一样的事情。如果能听到别人的故事和经历，那该多酷啊。

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:23.500
<v Ira Glass>And if you had to explain to somebody what are the worst things about middle school-- can I ask you to just walk me through it. What is so bad about middle school?
<v Ira Glass>如果你必须向别人解释初中最糟糕的事情是什么——我可以请你带我了解一下吗？初中到底哪里不好？

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:45.690
<v Annie>Kids there are all in socially awkward stages, that the drama every day can be frustrating. And girls write things that are someone likes so and so. And then no matter who you are, or what you do, you'll get made fun of for it. Anything, anything in the world you can get made fun of for.
<v Annie>那里的孩子都处在社交尴尬期，每天的戏码多到让人抓狂。女孩们会写一些关于某人喜欢谁谁谁的纸条。无论你是谁，或者你做什么，你都会因此被嘲笑。世界上任何事情，你都可能因此被嘲笑。

00:01:45.690 --> 00:02:01.540
<v Ira Glass>In Annie's case, she had friends who smoked, so she got criticized for smoking. But then she also was made fun of for not smoking, for being too much of a sissy to start smoking. She was made fun of for coming from a bilingual elementary school where everybody learned to speak Spanish and spoke it throughout the day.
<v Ira Glass>就安妮的情况而言，她有朋友抽烟，所以她因为抽烟被批评。但她也因为不抽烟而被嘲笑，被说成是个没胆量开始抽烟的胆小鬼。她还因为来自一所双语小学而被嘲笑，在那种学校里，每个人都学西班牙语，并且整天用西班牙语交流。

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:25.370
<v Annie>And leaving elementary school, I guess I thought that when I got to middle school, everyone would think it was really cool that I spoke Spanish, but when I got there, they mostly just thought it was dumb. I don't know if they were jealous or what. They would make fun of me for it. Then they'd say we were all full of ourselves, that we spoke different languages and stuff.
<v Annie>离开小学时，我以为到了初中，大家会觉得我会说西班牙语特别酷，但当我到了那里，他们大多觉得这很蠢。我不知道他们是嫉妒还是怎么的。他们会因此嘲笑我。然后他们会说我们自以为是，说我们讲些乱七八糟的语言之类的。

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:27.370
<v Ira Glass>Did it make you feel bad?
<v Ira Glass>这让你觉得难受吗？

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:53.090
<v Annie>Yeah, I didn't want to stick out in that way. If I got a new sweater or something, say for Christmas, that I really liked, and I would really want to wear it to school or something, but I'd be nervous because what if someone didn't like my sweater or someone made fun of me for wearing it. It can be hard to do even the smallest things, because you're so nervous that people tease you or judge you from it.
<v Annie>是的，我不想那样格格不入。如果我有一件新毛衣，比如说圣诞节收到的，我很喜欢，我很想穿到学校，但我会很紧张，万一有人不喜欢我的毛衣，或者有人因为我穿它而嘲笑我怎么办。即使做最小的事情也很难，因为你太紧张了，怕人们因为这些而取笑或评判你。

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:55.990
<v Ira Glass>That sweater example, is that a real example?
<v Ira Glass>那个毛衣的例子，是真的吗？

00:02:55.990 --> 00:03:09.030
<v Annie>Yeah, it actually is. I worried about it so much. I also had a pair of moccasins that I'd never worn, and they're kind of my signature now. Everybody really likes them. They're ankle-high, lace-up moccasins.
<v Annie>是的，确实是真的。我为此担心了很久。我还有一双从未穿过的莫卡辛鞋，现在它们算是我的标志了。大家都真的很喜欢它们。它们是脚踝高、系带的莫卡辛鞋。

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:11.830
<v Ira Glass>And how long did you have the moccasins before you actually wore them?
<v Ira Glass>那双鞋你放了多久才穿上？

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:27.030
<v Annie>A few months, probably two months. I guess I just thought if people didn't like them, they would all make fun of me for wearing them. And I didn't want to stand out that much.
<v Annie>几个月，大概两个月吧。我想我只是觉得如果人们不喜欢它们，他们会因为我穿它们而取笑我。我不想表现得那么引人注目。

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:30.990
<v Ira Glass>What could be done to make middle school better?
<v Ira Glass>有什么办法能让初中变得更好吗？

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:39.060
<v Annie>I don't think you can really do anything about it. [LAUGHS] Nothing.
<v Annie>我觉得你对此无能为力。[笑] 什么都做不了。

00:03:39.060 --> 00:04:00.420
<v Ira Glass>We talked about this for a little while. She said basically, everybody comes into middle school as a little kid, and you're going to have to grow up, and figure out who's in what group, and who you are, and who's above who. And you're going to have to figure that out somewhere at that age, right? It might as well be middle school. And it was terrible, she says, but now she's in high school.
<v Ira Glass>我们聊了一会儿。她说基本上每个人进入初中时都是个小孩子，你必须长大，弄清楚谁在哪个圈子里，你是谁，谁比谁地位高。在那个年纪，你总得在某个地方弄明白这些，对吧？那不如就在初中吧。她说那段经历很糟糕，但现在她上高中了。

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:11.450
<v Annie>Whatever middle school was, it worked. Everyone is a lot friendlier, and everyone's lives are a lot better now.
<v Annie>不管初中是什么样的，它都起作用了。现在大家友好多了，每个人的生活也都好多了。

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:45.260
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our radio program, for Annie we look at whatever it is that happens in those mysterious years that we call middle school. We have stories today from all over the country, people lurching their way through these years when you're figuring out so, so much. We go to middle school dances and classrooms, and down to the Mexican border. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life , distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>好了，今天在我们的广播节目中，为了安妮，我们将探究那些被称为“初中”的神秘岁月里到底发生了什么。今天我们有来自全国各地的故事，记录了人们在这些年里步履蹒跚地前行，而你在此期间会学到太多太多的东西。我们将走进初中的舞会和教室，一直到墨西哥边境。这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台，《美国生活》节目，由国际公共广播电台发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:04:45.260 --> 00:05:09.080
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Life in the Middle Ages. So we wanted to understand what is going on exactly physically and emotionally with middle school kids, and we talked to all kinds of people about that these last few weeks. And one of the producers on our staff had a very strong opinion about all this, Alex Blumberg. Before he got into radio, he taught in a middle school in Chicago for four years. He was a science teacher. And he concluded from that experience--
<v Ira Glass>第一幕：中世纪的生活。所以我们想了解初中生在生理和心理上到底发生了什么，过去几周我们采访了各种各样的人。我们员工中的一位制作人亚历克斯·布卢姆伯格对此有非常强烈的看法。在进入广播行业之前，他在芝加哥的一所初中教了四年书。他曾是一名科学老师。他从那段经历中得出结论——

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:22.380
<v Alex Blumberg>I don't know if they learned anything. They are so consumed with learning all these other lessons about where they fit in, in the social order, and how their bodies are now working and--
<v Alex Blumberg>我不知道他们是否学到了什么。他们太沉迷于学习其他那些关于他们在社会阶层中处于什么位置、身体正在发生什么变化的课程，以及——

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:22.790
<v Ira Glass>And who they're going to be.
<v Ira Glass>以及他们将来会成为什么样的人。

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:31.040
<v Alex Blumberg>--who they're attracted to, and who they're going to be, that facts and figures and geography, and all the other stuff that you teach in school, it just doesn't even penetrate.
<v Alex Blumberg>——他们对谁感兴趣，他们将来会成为什么样的人，以至于事实、数字、地理以及你在学校教的所有其他东西，根本就进不去他们的脑子。

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:38.220
<v Ira Glass>Wait, are you saying we shouldn't even bother to have them in school? We should just basically put them to work in the factory for two or three years?
<v Ira Glass>等等，你是说我们甚至不应该费心让他们去上学？我们应该基本上让他们去工厂工作两三年吗？

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:44.690
<v Alex Blumberg>Yeah. I basically came away thinking you're sort of wasting your time trying to teach middle school students anything.
<v Alex Blumberg>是的。我基本上得出的结论是，试图教初中生任何东西都是在浪费时间。

00:05:44.690 --> 00:06:05.110
<v Ira Glass>If that seems just insane, Alex told me that Maria Montessori, who's the creator of the Montessori schools who looked at what kids could learn at each developmental stage, at one point in her career actually tossed out a similar suggestion. Perhaps, she said, young adolescents would be better off at a farm school, what she called an Erdkinder, where they would work with the land.
<v Ira Glass>如果这听起来简直不可理喻，亚历克斯告诉我，蒙台梭利学校的创始人玛丽亚·蒙台梭利，她曾研究过孩子们在每个发育阶段能学到什么，她在职业生涯的某个阶段实际上提出了一个类似的建议。她说，也许青少年在农场学校会更好，她称之为“大地之子”（Erdkinder），在那里他们将与土地一起劳作。

00:06:05.110 --> 00:06:10.510
<v Marian Strok>[LAUGHING] Has he just got a great sense of humor or what?
<v Marian Strok>[笑] 他是幽默感太强了还是怎么回事？

00:06:10.510 --> 00:06:22.990
<v Ira Glass>I ran Alex's thoughts by Marian Strok, the principle of Evergreen Academy Middle School in Chicago, which was listed as one of the best middle schools in the country, a school to watch, by the National Forum to Accelerate Middle Grades Reform.
<v Ira Glass>我把亚历克斯的想法告诉了芝加哥常青学院初中的校长玛丽安·斯特罗克，该校被全国初中教育改革加速论坛列为全国最好的初中之一，是一所值得关注的学校。

00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:38.370
<v Marian Strok>I have to say, well, I obviously don't agree, OK? I think that we definitely have a way to stick knowledge in their heads. It's being creative about how we do it, and taking into consideration all of those other things. I'm not going to lie. It's not easy.
<v Marian Strok>我不得不说，嗯，我显然不同意，好吗？我认为我们绝对有办法把知识塞进他们的脑子里。关键在于我们要以创造性的方式来做，并考虑到所有其他因素。我不会撒谎。这并不容易。

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:48.150
<v Ira Glass>Can I ask you how much of the stereotype around students this age is true? Are you saying that the stereotype is true? You just have to deal with what it is?
<v Ira Glass>我可以问问你，关于这个年龄段学生的成见有多少是真的吗？你是说这种成见是真实的吗？你只能去应对它吗？

00:06:48.150 --> 00:07:23.470
<v Marian Strok>It is true, and you have to-- as the adult in the situation-- you have to find a way to work around it, find a way to help them to grow up. They won't have to suffer if we do things right. They won't have to suffer as much, let's say. I mean you're going to go through emotional changes, and your body's changing. That can't be helped. The raging hormones, that can't be helped. All of that can't be helped. It's the way that we react to it that will make such a difference in their lives. It's our flexibility. It's our concern. It's our listening to them. It's our hearing them out.
<v Marian Strok>这是真的，你必须——作为这种情况下的成年人——你必须找到一种方法来绕过它，找到一种方法来帮助他们成长。如果我们做得对，他们就不必受苦。或者说，他们不必受那么多苦。我的意思是，你必然会经历情感变化，你的身体在发生变化。这是不可避免的。荷尔蒙的激增，这是不可避免的。所有这些都是不可避免的。我们应对它的方式将会对他们的生活产生巨大的影响。关键在于我们的灵活性。在于我们的关心。在于我们倾听他们。在于我们听取他们的想法。

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:29.380
<v Ira Glass>And so what's actually happening developmentally in the bodies and brains of kids when they're in middle school?
<v Ira Glass>那么，当孩子们上初中时，他们的身体和大脑在发育上到底发生了什么？

00:07:29.380 --> 00:07:38.390
<v Linda Perlstein>Let me give the overview this way. The terrible 12s are a complete analog to the terrible twos. They're just not as cute, right?
<v Linda Perlstein>让我这样来概述。12岁是个可怕的年纪，完全对应着“可怕的两岁”。只是他们没那么可爱了，对吧？

00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:56.540
<v Ira Glass>This is Linda Perlstein. When she wrote a book called Not Much, Just Chillin': The Hidden Lives of Middle Schoolers, following five middle school students very closely for a full year, she also incidentally researched everything she could find out about what is going on developmentally with kids that age that makes them so different from other ages. And it's fascinating.
<v Ira Glass>这位是琳达·珀尔斯坦。当她写那本名为《不多，只是闲逛：初中生隐藏的生活》的书时，她非常密切地跟踪了五名初中生整整一年。顺便说一句，她还研究了关于那个年龄段孩子发育情况的所有资料，这使他们与其他年龄段的孩子如此不同。这非常迷人。

00:07:56.540 --> 00:08:11.450
<v Linda Perlstein>This is the time of biggest growth for a human being, aside from infancy. So your bones are growing faster than your muscles, so you can't actually sit still.
<v Linda Perlstein>除了婴儿期，这是人类生长最快的时期。所以你的骨骼生长速度超过了肌肉，导致你根本无法静坐。

00:08:11.450 --> 00:08:40.070
<v Linda Perlstein>But your brain, your gray matter-- during the middle school years, what happens in your early stages of puberty is this fast overproduction of brain cells and connections, far more than you actually need. And only some of them are going to survive puberty. This growth in your frontal cortex, it peaks at 11 for girls and 12 for boys. And then what happens is the cells just fight it out for survival. And the ones that last are the ones you exercise more.
<v Linda Perlstein>但是你的大脑，你的灰质——在初中几年里，青春期早期发生的是大脑细胞和连接的快速过量生产，远超你的实际需要。其中只有一部分能度过青春期。这种大脑前额皮质的生长，女孩在11岁、男孩在12岁达到顶峰。接下来发生的是，细胞为了生存而进行竞争。最后存活下来的，就是你锻炼得更多的那部分。

00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:49.760
<v Ira Glass>In other words, during those years, your brain turns you into you, the adult you. The stuff you don't exercise just kind of goes away. And what stays?
<v Ira Glass>换句话说，在那几年里，你的大脑把你变成了现在的你，成年后的你。你没有锻炼到的东西就消失了。那么什么留下了呢？

00:08:49.760 --> 00:09:15.450
<v Linda Perlstein>So if you think about what you learned at the early stages of puberty, I don't know what that was for you. For me it was tap dancing and French. It sticks. I know French much better than any language I learned after that, and not because I had a better teacher. I was learning it at the right time. I can still do tap dances, though I won't, that I learned when I was 12 or 13.
<v Linda Perlstein>所以，如果你回想一下你在青春期早期学到的东西——我不确定对你来说那是什么。对我来说是踢踏舞和法语。它留下来了。我掌握法语的程度远超我后来学的任何语言，这并不是因为我遇到了更好的老师。我是在正确的时间学习它的。尽管我不会跳了，但我还能跳出我在12或13岁时学的踢踏舞。

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:18.390
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, I can still do magic tricks that I learned at that age.
<v Ira Glass>是的，我还能表演我在那个年纪学的魔术。

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:32.440
<v Linda Perlstein>Exactly, it's embossed in your existence. It's this important time for your brain. It's this use it or lose it time.
<v Linda Perlstein>没错，它印在了你的生命里。这对你的大脑来说是一个重要的时期。这是一个“不用就废”的时期。

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:52.450
<v Ira Glass>Linda Perlstein agrees that all the stereotypes about middle schoolers, everything, in fact, that Annie complained about, all of that is true. But she says that there's a flip side that people don't usually talk about. Middle school is when kids open up to the world. It's when they think about bigger things. And they haven't formed their opinions on things yet. Everything is up for grabs, which is amazing to be around.
<v Ira Glass>琳达·珀尔斯坦同意关于初中生的所有刻板印象，事实上，安妮抱怨的所有事情都是真实的。但她说，人们通常不会谈论事情的另一面。初中是孩子们向世界敞开心扉的时候。这是他们思考更宏大事物的时候。而且他们还没有形成对事物的看法。一切皆有可能，这真的很棒。

00:09:52.450 --> 00:10:16.400
<v Linda Perlstein>I just think kids that age are fun. I like being around them. Middle schoolers are in that real nice spot of being interesting, but also being able to explain to you what's going on in their minds and why they do things they do. They're self-reflective. They're capable of self-reflection in a way that younger kids might not be.
<v Linda Perlstein>我只是觉得那个年纪的孩子很有趣。我喜欢和他们在一起。初中生处于一个非常好的阶段，既有趣，又能向你解释他们脑子里在想什么以及为什么要那样做。他们善于反思。他们具备那种更小的孩子可能不具备的反思能力。

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:28.670
<v Ira Glass>Older kids, she says, high school kids, by then they can be jaded or cynical. They're settling into who they are. In middle school, everything's new. You're probably picking your friends for the very first time.
<v Ira Glass>她说，大一点的孩子，也就是高中生，到那时他们可能已经变得愤世嫉俗或冷嘲热讽了。他们正在定型。在初中，一切都是新鲜的。你可能第一次选择你的朋友。

00:10:28.670 --> 00:10:58.980
<v Ira Glass>For the first time, they aren't just the kids on your block or your parents' friends' kids, or the kids who are around. And so you make a best friend one day, who then you drop two weeks later. You get obsessed with guitar or computers or dance for two weeks, and then two weeks later, you drop it. For everything that's awful about middle school, she says, that's the great thing about it. You experiment.
<v Ira Glass>这是第一次，他们不仅仅是你街坊邻居的孩子，或者是父母朋友的孩子，或者仅仅是周围的孩子。所以你今天交了一个最好的朋友，两周后又不理他了。你会在两周内痴迷于吉他、电脑或舞蹈，然后两周后又放弃了。她说，初中的所有可怕之处，正是它的伟大之处。你在实验。

00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:25.170
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, Stutter Steps. One good place to see the experiment that is life in middle school in action is a middle school dance. Last Friday, there were middle school dances all over the country, all at the same time, and we sent reporters to a half dozen of them to find out how kids were doing. They talked to them before the kids went inside to the field of battle. And, no surprise, we found a lot of stress, a lot of uncertainty.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕：踉跄的脚步。观察初中生活实验的一个好地方是初中舞会。上周五，全国各地的初中都在同一时间举办舞会，我们派记者去了六个舞会现场，看看孩子们表现如何。他们在孩子们进入战场之前与他们进行了交谈。不出所料，我们发现了很多压力和不确定性。

